  "source": {
    "display_title": "WCC Compiler Reduces Worst-Case Execution Times",
    "authors": [
      "Heiko Falk",
      "Paul Lokuciejewski"
    ],
    "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
    "record_display_title": "Untitled Source",
    "publication_venue": "Real-Time Systems",
    "doi": "10.1007/s11241-010-9101-x"
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Static WCET reduction depends on optimizing the worst-case execution path, because reducing a noncritical path or a local block can leave the global WCET unchanged or only partly reduced.",
      "short_title": "WCEP governs WCET savings",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "A program’s WCET is computed by finding the maximum path WCET for all feasible paths; the path leading to the WCET is called the worst-case execution path. The source example reduces one WCEP block by 40 cycles but lowers global WCET only from 205 to 195 cycles."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCC couples a C compiler to the aiT static WCET analyzer so that WCET data is produced transparently during compilation rather than by a separate manual post-compilation step.",
      "short_title": "WCC integrates static analysis",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "WCC produces a CRL2 file, invokes aiT transparently, and performs value, loop bound, cache, pipeline, and path analysis while the compiler user is unaware that timing analysis is running in the background."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCC’s back-annotation raises assembly-level WCET information to source-level ICD-C structures, enabling source-level optimizations to use WCET, WCEP membership, execution frequencies, cache misses, code size, and spill-code data.",
      "short_title": "Back annotation enables optimization",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The transformed back-annotation data include WCET for the entire program, functions, and basic blocks; WCEP membership; worst-case execution frequency per CFG edge; execution feasibility; I-cache misses per basic block; code size; and spill-code amount."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCC’s loop analyzer computes safe loop iteration bounds for most benchmark loops and exact bounds for the great majority, reducing reliance on manual flow facts.",
      "short_title": "Loop bounds become automatic",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Table 1 reports 96 benchmarks with 707 loops in total; 99% of loops are analyzable and 96% have exact iteration counts on average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Interprocedural slicing and polyhedral loop evaluation substantially reduce loop-analysis time for many benchmarks while preserving safe loop-bound analysis.",
      "short_title": "Slicing accelerates loop analysis",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Table 2 reports matmul falling from 8.4 s to 2.4 s with slicing and 0.8 s with polytope evaluation, while fft falls from 920.7 s to 119.7 s with slicing and 110.5 s with polytope evaluation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCET-aware procedure cloning improves WCET estimates by specializing functions whose data-dependent loop bounds become constant in cloned calling contexts, but it trades timing precision for code-size growth.",
      "short_title": "Cloning sharpens loop bounds",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "For mpeg2, procedure cloning reduces WCET estimates by 63.6% for standard cloning and 62.7% for WCET-aware cloning; epic has a 693.7% code-size increase after standard cloning and 357.9% after WCET-aware cloning."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCET-centric procedure positioning reduces instruction-cache conflict misses by reordering functions using static WCET-derived call frequencies rather than profiling input data.",
      "short_title": "Positioning reduces cache misses",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Figure 7 reports that greedy procedure positioning achieves an average WCET reduction of 10%, while the heuristic reduces WCET by 4% on average; combined cloning and positioning reaches reductions up to 64%."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ILP-based code scratchpad allocation lowers WCET by moving beneficial basic-block regions into fast program scratchpad memory with negligible code-size and solver overhead.",
      "short_title": "Code SPM reduces WCET",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Across 73 benchmarks, tiny program SPMs reduce WCET to 92.6% of the no-SPM WCET, and SPMs large enough for entire benchmarks reach 60% of original WCET; average code size increases by 0.02%."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ILP-based data scratchpad allocation lowers WCET by moving frequently accessed variables into fast data scratchpad memory without changing generated code size.",
      "short_title": "Data SPM reduces WCET",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "For petrinet, an 8-byte data SPM reduces WCET to 82.5% and fitting all global data reaches 69.7%; for the real TriCore 40 kB SPM, average WCET reaches 79.8% of original WCET."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "WCET-aware register allocation lowers WCET by avoiding spill code on the WCEP, even when this increases total spill code or text size outside the critical path.",
      "short_title": "Register allocation protects WCEP",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Across 46 benchmarks, WCET-aware register allocation achieves an average WCET of 68.8% of traditional graph coloring, reducing WCET by 31.2%, while text size increases by 29.8% on average."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The WCC optimizations compose: applying register allocation, code and data scratchpad allocation, and procedure positioning to matmult reduces WCET by more than 70 percent relative to WCC -O2.",
      "short_title": "Optimizations compose strongly",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "For matmult, the combined sequence reduces WCET from 775,720 cycles to 227,794 cycles, with register allocation saving 284,000 cycles, code SPM a further 120,000 cycles, data SPM another 135,000 cycles, and procedure positioning 8,000 cycles."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-wcep-local-savings-example--a6f3c91d4b2e",
      "title": "WCEP Local Savings Example",
      "excerpt": "The source defines program WCET as the maximum path WCET over feasible paths and names the responsible path the WCEP. In the example CFG, the original WCEP has 205 cycles. Reducing one WCEP basic block from 80 to 40 cycles changes the global WCET to 195 cycles, so the global saving is 10 cycles rather than the local 40-cycle saving.",
      "overview": "This formal example explains why WCET-aware compilation must reason globally about the worst-case execution path. It establishes that local timing gains do not automatically translate into equal whole-program WCET gains, because another path may become critical or partially absorb the local improvement.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.253-254"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-transparent-ait-invocation--b19e7f2a0c64",
      "title": "Transparent aiT Invocation",
      "excerpt": "WCC produces a CRL2 file for the program requiring WCET timing data, invokes aiT fully transparently, and performs value, loop bound, cache, pipeline, and path analysis. The compiler user is described as unaware that timing analysis is performed in the background.",
      "overview": "This passage is concrete process evidence for the compiler-analyzer integration. It shows that timing analysis is not a detached manual step; it is embedded in the compiler workflow and supplies the WCET data used by later optimization decisions.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
      "page_refs": [
        "Sect. 3.3",
        "p.261"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-back-annotation-data-list--c02d9e5a8f71",
      "title": "Back-Annotation Data Inventory",
      "excerpt": "The transformed back-annotation data are listed as WCET for the entire program, functions, and basic blocks; whether an ICD-C block lies on the WCEP; worst-case execution frequency per CFG edge; feasibility of blocks and edges; I-cache misses per basic block; code size; and spill code per assembly-level basic block.",
      "overview": "The inventory identifies the exact timing and structural facts lifted from low-level analysis into source-level ICD-C. This matters because high-level transformations such as cloning and positioning can only be WCET-aware if these optimization inputs are available at their abstraction level.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "2",
