    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-loop-analysis-table-one--d44c2a9fb703",
      "title": "Loop Precision Table",
      "excerpt": "Table 1 reports loop-analysis precision over MRTC, DSPStone, MediaBench/MiBench, UTDSP, and miscellaneous benchmarks. The total row covers 96 benchmarks and 707 loops, with 99% analyzable loops and 96% exact loop iteration counts.",
      "overview": "This table is the strongest quantitative evidence for the automatic flow-fact claim. It demonstrates broad benchmark coverage and distinguishes safe analyzability from exactness, while also showing that remaining imprecision is limited to a small minority of loops.",
      "evidence_type": "table_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
      "page_refs": [
        "Table 1",
        "p.271"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-loop-analysis-runtime-table--e7aa59c130d8",
      "title": "Loop Runtime Table",
      "excerpt": "Table 2 gives loop-analysis run times: matmul is 8.4 s basic, 2.4 s with slicing, and 0.8 s with polytope evaluation; fft is 920.7 s basic, 119.7 s with slicing, and 110.5 s with polytope evaluation; matrix1 is 0.8 s, 0.09 s, and 0.03 s respectively.",
      "overview": "These measured timings show that the auxiliary loop-analysis techniques address scalability, not merely precision. The examples also reveal scope limits: polyhedral evaluation helps when its prerequisites apply, while other benchmarks may fall back to the iterative analysis.",
      "evidence_type": "table_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4"
