        "pp.279-280"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-code-spm-results--ad73e62b9c50",
      "title": "Program SPM Results",
      "excerpt": "The code SPM evaluation applies an ILP to 73 real-life benchmarks. For g721_encode, a 10% program SPM yields 71% of original WCET and a fully fitting SPM yields 52.2%. Across all benchmarks, tiny SPMs reach 92.6% of no-SPM WCET and full-fit SPMs reach 60%, with average code-size growth of 0.02%.",
      "overview": "This evidence quantifies the effect of allocating code to fast scratchpad memory. It supports the claim that WCET-aware memory placement can produce large reductions while keeping code growth and ILP solving overhead negligible for the evaluated benchmark set.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig. 10",
        "Fig. 11",
        "pp.286-288"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-data-spm-results--0f48d6aa271e",
      "title": "Data SPM Results",
      "excerpt": "For petrinet, an 8-byte data SPM yields 82.5% of original WCET, 32 bytes yields 71.4%, and fitting all global data yields 69.7%. For fsm, 8 bytes yields 93.5% and 256 bytes or larger yields 78.6%. With the real TriCore 40 kB SPM, average WCET reaches 79.8% of original WCET.",
      "overview": "The data scratchpad evidence shows that WCET improvements come from selecting high-value variables, not from changing instruction sequences. It also marks the architecture-specific scope: the reported gains rely on TriCore memory characteristics and on benchmarks with static or global data.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "8"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig. 12",
        "Fig. 13",
        "pp.290-292"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-register-and-combined-results--6c5f58db924a",
      "title": "Register and Combined Results",
      "excerpt": "Across 46 benchmarks, WCET-aware register allocation achieves 68.8% of the original WCET on average, a 31.2% reduction, while spectral reaches 24.1% and qurt reaches 93.1%. For matmult, the combined sequence reduces WCET from 775,720 cycles to 227,794 cycles, an overall reduction of more than 70%.",
      "overview": "This evidence covers both a single optimization and the composition claim. Register allocation demonstrates WCEP-sensitive spill placement, while matmult demonstrates that multiple WCET-aware optimizations can add constructively rather than merely duplicating the same savings.",
      "evidence_type": "observational_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "9",
